# A Bratislava new-build costs what two do in Martin. And prices are falling nowhere

Slovakia's new-build supply is {totalAvailable} available flats in {totalProjects} projects across {towns} towns, half of them in Bratislava. The median flat on offer costs {baPrice} € in Bratislava, {nrPrice} € in Nitra and {mtPrice} € in Martin. The unit price is {baScopeMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in Bratislava and {restMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in the rest of the country. Of {moves90} price-list changes over the last {stepDays} days, {movesUpPctEn}% were increases.

As of {asOfEn} the supply was {totalAvailable} available flats in {totalProjects} projects across {towns} towns. Bratislava holds {baAvailable} of them, or {baSharePctEn}% of the country; the remaining {restAvailable} are spread across {restTowns} towns. The largest regional markets are Nitra with {nrAvailable} flats, Trnava with {ttAvailable}, Košice with {keAvailable}, Prešov with {poAvailable} and Poprad with {ppAvailable}.

The median unit price is {baScopeMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in Bratislava and {restMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in the rest of the country. The order behind Bratislava does not follow the size of the town: Košice is second at {keM2} €/m² incl. VAT, Žilina third at {zaM2} and Banská Bystrica fourth at {bbM2} — above Trnava ({ttM2}), Trenčín ({tnM2}) and Nitra ({nrM2}). The lowest unit price among the {tableTowns} towns with a larger supply is {cheapestM2Town} at {lcM2} €/m² incl. VAT.

The median absolute price of a flat is {baPrice} € in Bratislava at {baAreaEn} m². That is roughly twice the median in Martin ({mtPrice} €) and almost three times the one in Lučenec ({lcPrice} €). The ranking by flat price and the ranking by price per metre diverge. Piešťany has the {psM2RankAsc}th cheapest metre ({psM2} €/m² incl. VAT), yet a median flat of {psPrice} € — higher than Trnava or Nitra, because at {psAreaEn} m² its flats are the largest in the table. Banská Bystrica is the opposite: the {bbM2RankDesc}th dearest metre, yet at {bbAreaEn} m² and a median of {bbPrice} € the cheapest of the eight regional capitals.

