## Method

Residata reads Slovak developers' price lists every day, flat by flat. This overview rests on {totalProjects} projects across {towns} towns and on observations since {windowFromEn}, that is {windowDays} days.

**What is counted.** Available flats are those the developer lists as available; reserved and sold are excluded. Only projects whose price list we currently read are included, and towns with at least {mapMinStock} available flats; the table shows towns with at least {tableMinStock}. Homes only — garages, parking spaces, cellars and commercial units are excluded from every figure. The median €/m² is computed on living area including VAT; these are asking prices. Bencont and BuiltMind publish a mean and work with usable area, so their unit price and ours are not directly comparable.

**Months to clear** is stock divided by the average monthly number of sales over the observed period; a sale is identified from a change of status in the developer's own price list. Reserved flats count as stock rather than as sales, and their share is higher outside Bratislava, so the regional figures are conservative in that respect.

**A price change** compares the same flat with its own earlier price, at one reading per flat per day and a change of at least two hundred euros.

**Coverage check.** The rezido.sk register lists {covIndexProjects} Slovak projects, {covSoldOut} of them sold out. A comparison against our catalogue on {asOfEn} found {covMissingProjects} selling projects we do not track, holding {covMissingUnits} available flats, or {covMissingPctEn}% of supply.

**Check against published figures.** {benchSource} reports a Bratislava supply of {benchSupply} flats for {benchPeriod}; on a comparable definition we get {ourSupplyComparable}, a difference of {supplyDeltaPctEn}%. Their average asking price per flat is {benchMeanPrice} € and ours {ourMeanPrice} €, a difference of {meanPriceDeltaPctEn}%.

